.he "less (1)                     8/16/26                     less (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
less
- a pager that goes backward
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
less [ option ] [ +cmd ] [ file ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Less is a pager, like more, but it can move backward as well as
forward through the file, and it starts before reading the whole
input, so a large file comes up fast.  A command may be preceded by a
number, called N below, that some commands use.
.sp
With no file, less reads the standard input.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
COMMANDS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
h	Show a summary of the commands.

.ti -10
space or f	Scroll forward N lines, default one screen.

.ti -10
b	Scroll backward N lines, default one screen.

.ti -10
return or j	Scroll forward one line; y or k scrolls backward one line.

.ti -10
d and u	Scroll forward and backward N lines, default ten.

.ti -10
g and G	Go to line N, default the beginning and the end.

.ti -10
/pattern and ?pattern	Search forward and backward for the N-th line with pattern.

.ti -10
n	Repeat the last search.

.ti -10
m c	Mark the position with letter c; ' c returns to it.

.ti -10
E [ file ]	Examine a new file; N and P step to the next and previous file.

.ti -10
- c	Toggle the setting of command line option c.

.ti -10
r or ^L	Repaint the screen.

.ti -10
= or ^G	Print the file name, byte offset and, if known, the length.

.ti -10
v	Edit the current file, with the editor named by EDITOR or vi.

.ti -10
! command	Run a shell command.

.ti -10
q	Quit.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-c	Assume the data is clean; do not repaint so much.

.ti -10
-d	Run as if on a hardcopy terminal.

.ti -10
-e	Quit when the end of the file is reached, rather than ringing the bell.

.ti -10
-h N	Never scroll more than N lines backward at once.

.ti -10
-p	Repaint by scrolling from the bottom of the screen.

.ti -10
-x N	Set tab stops every N columns, default 8.

.ti -10
-s	Squeeze runs of blank lines down to one.

.ti -10
-t	Start a forward search from the bottom of the screen rather than the top.

.ti -10
-w	Do not print ~ on the lines after the end of the file.

.ti -10
-m and -M	Use the medium or the long prompt, which shows the percent into the file.

.ti -10
-q and -Q	Quiet the bell at the end of the file, or everywhere.

.ti -10
-u and -U	Show underlined text as underline, overstrike, or ^H.

.ti -10
-z N	Set the scroll window to N lines, default 24.

.ti -10
-b[f|p] N	Set the number of read-ahead buffers.

.ti -10
-P[m|M] string	Set the prompt string, with letters F (file), b (byte), p (percent) and O (file n of n).

.ti -10
-l file	Write the input to file as it is read, as a log.

.ti -10
+cmd	Run cmd each time a new file is opened.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
Options may also be read from the environment variable LESS, and a
- command inside less toggles them one at a time.
.sp
The port asks the terminal its size, so it knows how many lines to a
screen; on a terminal that cannot answer, the default is 24.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
more (1), vi (1)
